WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Jev Foundry Judge: Jev as the judge for Azure AI Foundry agent evaluation

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.600
Step 1 · Bring your own Jev key. It stays in this browser tab and is never stored

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.500
Step 2 · Start from a synthetic scenario: a support agent with tool calls

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.400
Or bring your own traces. The format guide lists the fields each metric needs

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.300
Download the starter template, then upload it: preview first, nothing changes yet

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:30.700
The inspector shows the exact case Apply will add: request, answer, tool definitions

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:35.200
9 cases, each with the author's 1–5 labels per metric

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.100
Step 3 · Run Jev next to Foundry's built-in LLM judge, all four metrics

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:49.025
One Jev call per conversation answers every metric. Both judges run in one Foundry evaluate() call

00:00:49.025 --> 00:00:52.825
Step 4 · Dashboard: every number comes from the calls just made on this run

00:00:52.825 --> 00:00:57.525
Cost and time on the same workload: only rows where both judges scored the same metrics

00:00:57.525 --> 00:01:03.425
Agreement with the labels, per metric, with its own n. Synthetic author labels, not a general claim

00:01:03.425 --> 00:01:10.625
Step 5 · Export results, the evaluated dataset and a benchmark brief

00:01:10.625 --> 00:01:14.125
Then run the same evaluator classes in your own Foundry project

00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:17.158
Jev Foundry Judge · bring your own key · MIT licensed
